			<div class="content" id="post-content-4541193">
									

<p>Mainly I think you should go over the dialogue again. The characters feels somewhat one dimensional and to a certain extent even cartoonish in a way that makes reading their interaction boring. I understand the cartoonish behavior from the anomaly itself, but when it's the case with all of the characters, it gets kind of old. Also, this is more of a personal nitpick, but I find the repeating 'over' in the end of each line jarring to read. I don't see why the Foundation couldn't have a continuous live audio feed.</p>
<p>I feel like one of the reasons I feel this way is that there is very little straight description of what's happening. The readers have to form a mental image out of dialogue alone, the result of which is that the characters aren't animated enough.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">A&nbsp;few&nbsp;examples&nbsp;that&nbsp;I&nbsp;could&nbsp;pick&nbsp;up</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Dam:</strong> Hannah Dam checking in. Just finished inspecting the surf, there’s a trail of seaweed dragged out of the water and onto the sand. But it stops right by a crater in the sand, like someone buried a grenade after pulling the pin, over.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>She's explaining something that she has done something after it's already done, skipping the action entirely. She's just kinda standing there and explaining because there's no indication of anything else and the reader has to be like &quot;well I guess that happened then&quot;. Also seems silly to me that she would check in only after she's done doing her thing, even though Command just asked everyone to speak up. It leaves like an odd feeling gap where Command speaks, then Dam does her thing, speaks and only then everyone else speaks up.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Jefferson:</strong> Do they? I’ve never heard a metaphor that worked as intended. Similes, on the other hand-</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Seems odd to me that he would just go on a random tangent about metaphors and similes in the middle of a mission when they have nothing to do with anything.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Ekblad:</strong> Enough. There’s an arm sticking out of the beach here.</p>
<p><strong>Bjugstad:</strong> Anybody attached?</p>
<p><strong>Ekblad:</strong> Negative. Only identifying marks… paint on the remaining nails, with ring tan on a finger. Let’s move out. Nothing more we can do here. We can inspect the scene after the skip is inert. Command, you reading us still, over?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Again, there's no action described here so its kind of like they all just stand there and talk.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Ekblad:</strong> I’ve got visual contact. Directly ahead of us.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Ahead of them where? On the street? On a sidestreet? I don't know. In a sense, the picture that is being painted here is incomplete, which breaks immersion.</p>
<p>As for formatting goes, you forgot to fix this part:</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Ekblad:</strong> Command, this is Commander Calvin Ekblad, we’ve arrived on the beach, over.<br />
Command: Confirmed. Check in, all agents, over.</p>
</blockquote>
